      ],
      "publication_year": 1982,
      "publication_date": "1982-10-01",
      "abstract": "Progress as one idea in the history of ideas or as the definitive idea of history itself is, no matter how grandly conceived, in- volved in the vicissitudes of everyday life. Progress, as it is felt and understood by the individual, is inevitably conflated with the discourses of theology, philosophy, science, literature, and politi- cal theory. This insight into the relationship of history and consciousness, mediated by language, is credited to Giambattista Vico's New Science. Antonio Gramsci, in his multilayered analysis of culture and society, saw progress similarly but with an emphasis on class. For him, progress was an idea whose history was bound to those social groups and classes who benefitted from its influence. In this sense, progress was associated, for Gramsci, with Liberal historiography as present above all in the works of Benedetto Croce, and with the Italian bourgeoisie whose interests it had served. But Gramsci had his own commitments to progress.",
